| 1 | 3 | Music for a reason. *Why do people choose the music they do, and what in a piece shapes how we hear it?* | 16 | mu-g3-choose; mu-g3-respond | MU:Pr4.1.3a; MU:Re7.1.3a; MU:Cn10.1.3a; MU:Re7.2.3a; MU:Re9.1.3a; MU:Cn11.1.3a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: Black Lodge Singers (a powwow drum group of Blackfeet and Yakama singers, Washington state; the unit plan records whether active, as of its date), Kids' Pow-Wow Songs (1996), intertribal social songs, described and linked to the group's or the label's page; Toumani DiabatAc (Mali, 1965 to 2024) and BallakAc Sissoko (Mali, born 1967; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), New Ancient Strings (1999), two kora players from griot families, described and linked to the label's page; the class's song menu: "Cielito Lindo" (Mexico, Quirino Mendoza y CortAcs, 1882, public domain), "Sur le pont d'Avignon" (France, traditional) and "Hine e Hine" (Aotearoa New Zealand, Princess Te Rangi Pai, 1907, public domain) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: an explained pick (mu-g2-choose) and a concept described for a purpose (mu-g2-respond). MU:Pr4.1.3a: choose the class's song to perform from the three-song menu and show how interest, what the class knows, its purpose and where it will be sung shape the pick (the chosen song is the concert song of unit 4). MU:Re7.1.3a: show and describe, on a listening-log page, how a piece chosen for listening reflects interests, experiences and purposes. MU:Cn10.1.3a: show how the student's own tastes, knowledge and skills shape a choice in music, or an invented or public person's, marked the same. MU:Re7.2.3a: describe how a piece's form (verse and chorus, call and answer), its elements (beat, loud and soft, high and low) and where it is heard (a powwow, a concert hall, a lullaby at home) shape the response. MU:Re9.1.3a: judge a piece or a performance with a set class checklist and say how it fits its setting. MU:Cn11.1.3a: show how music relates to another art (the kora's story-telling, the powwow's dancing), a school subject or daily life. Technique: listening in a set routine with a listening map; the class song learned by ear in unison in the head voice. Words: setting, purpose, form, verse, chorus, criteria. Showing: the chosen song sung for a partner class with the class's reasons said. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.3a, a kept card: the class's pick and three reasons (interest, knowledge, purpose or setting), with the student's own reason added; MU:Re7.1.3a, a listening-log page: how one chosen piece reflects an interest, an experience or a purpose; MU:Cn10.1.3a, a talk item: one taste, piece of knowledge or skill and the music choice it shapes (own, invented or public, marked the same); MU:Re7.2.3a, a written item on an unseen open recording: one feature of form or an element and the setting, each tied to the response; MU:Re9.1.3a, a written item on the same recording: the class checklist marked and one sentence on its fit to the setting; MU:Cn11.1.3a, a talk item: one tie between an unseen piece and another art, subject or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: note values as parts of a bar (unit 4); explaining why a setting changes a response (grade 4). Tools: the listening map, the class checklist card, the song menu cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own; a listening-map player on the teacher device or the printed map. Free path: the voice and the printed map. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First because every later unit needs the listening routine, the listening log and the class checklist (the listening-log and describe-interpret-judge models are retrieved and widened here), and because the song chosen here is the one the class reads and performs in unit 4. Listening opens the year so the class hears music in its setting before making it. Six ids, but most are heard and written, so 16 days | Candidate: a check that the three menu songs give the class a real choice of setting (a lullaby, a dance song, a game song) | Powwow songs are heard by listening only, never sung, drummed or danced by the class, and no student imitates regalia; the sensitivity agent first reads the group's and the label's pages and a Blackfeet or intertribal powwow organization's published guidance on which songs are social. The kora: the agent reads the label's page and a Mande cultural source on griot families. "Hine e Hine" is sung in te reo M�?ori: the agent reads a M�?ori source for its history and pronunciation before the unit keeps it. Own-taste tasks have an invented or public option. Hazards and controls: recordings at a set low level; voice rest and water; no student sings alone. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 3 | Two ways to play it. *How do performers use loudness and speed to show what a piece means, and how do we make up music that fits a setting?* | 12 | mu-g3-invent; mu-g3-express | MU:Cr1.1.3a; MU:Cr1.1.3b; MU:Pr4.3.3a; MU:Re8.1.3a | Creating (Cr1); performing (Pr4); responding (Re8). Works: Claude Debussy, "Clair de lune" from Suite bergamasque (France, published 1905), a public domain score in two recordings by different pianists whose rights lines the unit plan checks; Hua Yanjun, known as Abing (China, 1893 to 1950), "Erquan Yingyue" (The Moon Reflected in the Second Spring), for erhu, recorded in 1950, described and linked to a museum's or an archive's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: improvising on purpose inside a given tonality and meter (mu-g2-invent) and two performers compared (mu-g2-express). MU:Cr1.1.3a: improvise rhythm and melody ideas on a five-note scale and describe how they fit a purpose and a setting (a game, a lullaby, a march). MU:Cr1.1.3b: invent rhythm and melody ideas inside a tonality (major or minor) or meter (twos or threes) the student chooses. MU:Pr4.3.3a: try a song with different loudness and speed and show, with a spoken reason, the version that carries its meaning. MU:Re8.1.3a: describe how two recorded performers use loudness and speed in their readings of one piece. Technique: the pentatonic call and answer, improvised on the voice or the tuned bottles; planned loudness and speed marked on the expression map. Words: improvise, tonality, meter, loudness, speed, reading. Showing: each group's two readings of one class song, played for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.3a, a performance item at the teacher's spot: a four-beat phrase improvised for a purpose card, with one sentence on how it fits; MU:Cr1.1.3b, a guide look-for on the kept idea-bank card and its sound-only recording: an idea inside the tonality or meter the student chose and named; MU:Pr4.3.3a, a guide look-for on the group's reading: the planned loudness and speed carried out, with the student's spoken reason; MU:Re8.1.3a, a written item on two unseen readings of one open-licensed tune: one loudness or speed difference and what each shows. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tone color (grade 4), harmony (grade 4). Tools: the expression map, the purpose cards and the idea-bank card, Curio Class's own; a free on-screen keyboard or the tuned bottles. Free path: the voice. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 1 because comparing performers' readings uses its listening routine and setting words; improvising for a setting needs the setting words just taught. Before unit 3 because a composed piece starts from the idea bank. One technique and four ids, so 12 days | Candidate: a third reading by a living performer, described and linked, for range | Abing was a blind street musician; his blindness is named only as his own era's published sources name it, and the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or archive's page first. Hazards and controls: tuned bottles plastic, filled and capped by an adult and taped shut; recordings at a set low level; no student improvises alone in front of the class (pairs or the teacher's spot). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
