nt families with a listening map; the concert piece learned by ear. Words: context, culture, technical skill, explain, criteria, instrument family. Showing: the chosen piece sung for a partner class with the class's argument for it. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.4a, a kept card: the pick and the reasons, one of them the class's skill; MU:Re7.1.4a, a listening-log page: how one chosen piece reflects a purpose or a context, explained; MU:Cn10.1.4a, a talk item: one taste, piece of knowledge or skill and the choice it shapes (own, invented or public, marked the same); MU:Re7.2.4a, a written item on an unseen open recording: how its form or an element and its setting shape the response, with a "because"; MU:Re9.1.4a, a written item on the same recording: set criteria marked and why it fits or does not fit its setting; MU:Cn11.1.4a, a talk item: one tie between an unseen piece and another art, subject or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: evidence cited from the music (grade 5). Tools: the listening map, the criteria card, the menu cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: the printed map. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 4 because it reopens the listening routine and the log after the summer and widens setting into culture, which units 6 and 8 use; the piece chosen here is unit 8's concert piece | Candidate: a check that each menu song's verses and history are clear before the class sings it | Dvo�TA�k's own claims about African American spirituals and Native American music as sources are told as his claims, with Harry T. Burleigh, who sang spirituals for him, named; the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or a scholarly page first. "Shenandoah": the agent checks its verses and the class sings a checked set. AlHaj left Iraq as a refugee: told in one plain sentence from his own page, read first by the agent, and no student is asked about their own family's travels. Own-taste tasks have an invented or 