Each check day: the class writes its card and then works in its listening logs with no instruments while the teacher
hears six groups of five for about four minutes each; set-up and clean-up fit the rest of the 40 minutes. Every
student's evidence is collected once in a window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are band course days (year
4 starts on day 65, year 5 on day 129).

## Cross-subject

Three music rows of grades 3 to 5 lean on math and two on English language arts (data/progressions/arts.csv). All
are core courses (lesson 32). Each lean has its "if later" line.

### Mathematics, grades 3 to 5 (same year)

| Used here | Row and contact | Earliest unit of math that teaches it | If math has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 4 (year 3, days 49 to 64 of the year, school weeks 25 to 32): note values as parts of a whole bar | mu-g3-read, math:3.NF.A.1 | math grade 3 unit 6 (days 92 to 114), weeks 19 to 23 | The unit reads rhythms by beat counts and syllables only (a quarter note one beat, two eighths one beat shared), as units 1 to 3 do, and names no note value as a fraction |
| Unit 8 (year 4, weeks 25 to 32): equal note values, two quarters in one half | mu-g4-read, math:4.NF.A.1 | math grade 4 unit 2 (days 9 to 25), weeks 2 to 5 | Taught long before; if a school runs math in another order, beats are counted only |
| Unit 12 (year 5, weeks 25 to 32): mixed note values added within a bar | mu-g5-read, math:5.NF.A.1 | math grade 5 unit 9 (days 119 to 134), weeks 24 to 27 | The unit counts in whole and half beats (an eighth is half a beat, a dotted quarter one and a half), which is like-denominator adding from math grade 4 unit 3 (4.NF.B.3), and names mixed values as unlike fractions only after math's unit 9 |

Before unit 4 each year, written rhythms are beat counts and syllables only; time signatures in year 4 are beats a
bar, not fractions; no unit asks for measuring, percent or scale (lesson 13).

### English language arts, grades 3 to 5

| Used here | Row and contact | Where ELA teaches it | If ELA has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 1 (year 3, weeks 1 to 8): class talk about what was heard | mu-g3-respond, ela:SL.3.1 | english-language-arts grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 39), the same weeks | The music unit's talk card shows the turn rules (listen, one at a time, build on what was said) and the teacher models them on the first listening day |
| Unit 9 (year 5, weeks 1 to 8): an opinion supported by reasons, in writing | mu-g5-respond, ela:W.5.1 | english-language-arts grade 5 unit 3 (days 84 to 123), after this unit | The unit uses the grade 4 opinion paragraph (W.4.1, english-language-arts grade 4 unit 3, days 81 to 119, the year before) and teaches its own claim-and-evidence frame, with evidence taken from the music; W.5.1 is practiced, not owned (see Row changes) |

Talk about works and every critique practice collaborative conversation (SL.3.1, SL.4.1, SL.5.1): practiced, not
owned.

### Social studies, grades 3 to 5

Works are taught with their time and place (bar criterion 7), using social studies' sourcing habits (STANDARD.md 6).
No row leans on a social studies row; the music units teach the setting they use themselves, on work cards, so no
lean either way.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks.** This frame's checks (weeks 15 and 24 in every year) stay off every core check week.
   Read against `frame.py calendar` on 2026-11-25: week 15 also holds health 3 to 5 (grades 3 and 5) and social and
   emotional learning 3 to 5 (grades 4 and 5); week 24 holds no other check in any of the three grades. Left for the lead's one calendar pass; weeks 1 to 7 are free of
   every check in all three grades today, but no year's first two units fit before week 8.
2. **Shape differs from music K to 2.** That frame reads in its first unit and revises in its concert unit; this
   one reads in its concert unit (the grade 3 reading row waits on math grade 3's fractions unit, weeks 19 to 23) and
   revises in its composing unit. The handoffs are row to row, so nothing breaks. Pick: keep both shapes and say so on
   the two course pages; the grades 6 to 8 writer chooses from the rows.
3. **Words lines against the national text.** The pilot's pattern notes ask each id's action to be checked against
   the national standard, not only the words file. This frame was written from the words file and the rows, with no
   download. Pick: the lead opens the music "at a glance" handout in Claude in Chrome and spot-checks the 63 grade 3
   to 5 lines (above all MU:Re7.1, MU:Re9.1 and the Pr4.2 c parts), before the year 3 scope.
4. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional costs: $30 (year 3), $25 (year 4), $25 (year 5) a class of 30,
   all optional beside a free path, plus $20 once for rhythm sticks where a school has none. Pick: the same ceiling
   the visual arts K to 2 frame proposes ($75 a class a year), with every unit's free path kept.
5. **Stagger key.** All five arts are subject `arts` in calendar.csv; each after-core course counts on its own in the
   lead's pass (frame-write, "After-core stagger"), so the other four arts 3 to 5 frames' weeks need checking against
   these.
