c relates to another art (the kora's story-telling, the powwow's dancing), a school subject or daily life. Technique: listening in a set routine with a listening map; the class song learned by ear in unison in the head voice. Words: setting, purpose, form, verse, chorus, criteria. Showing: the chosen song sung for a partner class with the class's reasons said. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.3a, a kept card: the class's pick and three reasons (interest, knowledge, purpose or setting), with the student's own reason added; MU:Re7.1.3a, a listening-log page: how one chosen piece reflects an interest, an experience or a purpose; MU:Cn10.1.3a, a talk item: one taste, piece of knowledge or skill and the music choice it shapes (own, invented or public, marked the same); MU:Re7.2.3a, a written item on an unseen open recording: one feature of form or an element and the setting, each tied to the response; MU:Re9.1.3a, a written item on the same recording: the class checklist marked and one sentence on its fit to the setting; MU:Cn11.1.3a, a talk item: one tie between an unseen piece and another art, subject or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: note values as parts of a bar (unit 4); explaining why a setting changes a response (grade 4). Tools: the listening map, the class checklist card, the song menu cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own; a listening-map player on the teacher device or the printed map. Free path: the voice and the printed map. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First because every later unit needs the listening routine, the listening log and the class checklist (the listening-log and describe-interpret-judge models are retrieved and widened here), and because the song chosen here is the one the class reads and performs in unit 4. Listening opens the year so the class hears music in its setting before making it. Six ids, but most are heard and written, so 16 days | Candidate: a check that the three menu songs give