| 2 | 3 | Two ways to play it. *How do performers use loudness and speed to show what a piece means, and how do we make up music that fits a setting?* | 12 | mu-g3-invent; mu-g3-express | MU:Cr1.1.3a; MU:Cr1.1.3b; MU:Pr4.3.3a; MU:Re8.1.3a | Creating (Cr1); performing (Pr4); responding (Re8). Works: Claude Debussy, "Clair de lune" from Suite bergamasque (France, published 1905), a public domain score in two recordings by different pianists whose rights lines the unit plan checks; Hua Yanjun, known as Abing (China, 1893 to 1950), "Erquan Yingyue" (The Moon Reflected in the Second Spring), for erhu, recorded in 1950, described and linked to a museum's or an archive's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: improvising on purpose inside a given tonality and meter (mu-g2-invent) and two performers compared (mu-g2-express). MU:Cr1.1.3a: improvise rhythm and melody ideas on a five-note scale and describe how they fit a purpose and a setting (a game, a lullaby, a march). MU:Cr1.1.3b: invent rhythm and melody ideas inside a tonality (major or minor) or meter (twos or threes) the student chooses. MU:Pr4.3.3a: try a song with different loudness and speed and show, with a spoken reason, the version that carries its meaning. MU:Re8.1.3a: describe how two recorded performers use loudness and speed in their readings of one piece. Technique: the pentatonic call and answer, improvised on the voice or the tuned bottles; planned loudness and speed marked on the expression map. Words: improvise, tonality, meter, loudness, speed, reading. Showing: each group's two readings of one class song, played for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.3a, a performance item at the teacher's spot: a four-beat phrase improvised for a purpose card, with one sentence on how it fits; MU:Cr1.1.3b, a guide look-for on the kept idea-bank card and its sound-only recording: an idea inside the tonality or meter the student chose and named; MU:Pr4.3.3a, a guide look-for on the group's reading: the planned loudness and speed carried out, with the student's spoken reason; MU:Re8.1.3a, a written item on two unseen readings of one open-licensed tune: one loudness or speed difference and what each shows. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tone color (grade 4), harmony (grade 4). Tools: the expression map, the purpose cards and the idea-bank card, Curio Class's own; a free on-screen keyboard or the tuned bottles. Free path: the voice. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 1 because comparing performers' readings uses its listening routine and setting words; improvising for a setting needs the setting words just taught. Before unit 3 because a composed piece starts from the idea bank. One technique and four ids, so 12 days | Candidate: a third reading by a living performer, described and linked, for range | Abing was a blind street musician; his blindness is named only as his own era's published sources name it, and the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or archive's page first. Hazards and controls: tuned bottles plastic, filled and capped by an adult and taped shut; recordings at a set low level; no student improvises alone in front of the class (pairs or the teacher's spot). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
