ance and its expression map: loudness, speed and tone color as planned, with the student's spoken reason; MU:Re8.1.4a, a written item on an unseen open recording: one tone color choice and what it carries. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: chords (grade 5), articulation (grade 5). Tools: the expression map, the drone card and the idea-bank card, Curio Class's own; the free on-screen keyboard for a drone, or a sung drone. Free path: the voice and the tuned bottles. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 5 because explaining a performer's choices uses its explaining words; before unit 7 because the harmony written down there starts from the drone and ostinato ideas here. One technique and four ids, so 12 days | Candidate: a living drone tradition from Europe (a Highland bagpipe or a hurdy-gurdy player), described and linked, for range | Ulali's song: the sensitivity agent reads the group's own page and each singer's published words first, and checks that the song is the group's own, not a ceremonial song; no student imitates the drum heartbeat as a Native drum, and the class's own drone is its own. Hindustani music: a raga can carry devotional roots; the agent reads the artist's page, and the class studies it by listening only. Hazards and controls: a held drone sung softly with breaths taken in turn; headphones, if used with the keyboard tool, at a capped volume and never shared; hands washed after shared instruments. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
