| 1 | 3 | Music for a reason. *Why do people choose the music they do, and what in a piece shapes how we hear it?* | 16 | mu-g3-choose; mu-g3-respond | MU:Pr4.1.3a; MU:Re7.1.3a; MU:Cn10.1.3a; MU:Re7.2.3a; MU:Re9.1.3a; MU:Cn11.1.3a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: Black Lodge Singers (a powwow drum group of Blackfeet and Yakama singers, Washington state; the unit plan records whether active, as of its date), Kids' Pow-Wow Songs (1996), intertribal social songs, described and linked to the group's or the label's page; Toumani Diabat� (Mali, 1965 to 2024) and Ballak� Sissoko (Mali, born 1967; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), New Ancient Strings (1999), two kora players from griot families, described and linked to the label's page; the class's song menu: "Cielito Lindo" (Mexico, Quirino Mendoza y Cort�s, 1882, public domain), "Sur le pont d'Avignon" (France, traditional) and "Hine e Hine" (Aotearoa New Zealand, Princess Te Rangi Pai, 1907, public domain) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: an explained pick (mu-g2-choose) and a concept described for a purpose (mu-g2-respond). MU:Pr4.1.3a: choose the class's song to perform from the three-song menu and show how interest, what the class knows, its purpose and where it will be sung shape the pick (the chosen song is the concert song of unit 4). MU:Re7.1.3a: show and describe, on a listening-log page, how a piece chosen for listening reflects interests, experiences and purposes. MU:Cn10.1.3a: show how the student's own tastes, knowledge and skills shape a choice in music, or an invented or public person's, marked the same. MU:Re7.2.3a: describe how a piece's form (verse and chorus, call and answer), its elements (beat, loud and soft, high and low) and where it is heard (a powwow, a concert hall, a lullaby at home) shape the response. MU:Re9.1.3a: judge a piece or a performance with a set class checklist and say how it fits its setting. MU:Cn11.1.3a: show how music relates to another art (the kora's story-telling, the powwow's dancing), a school subject or daily life. Technique: listening in a set routine with a listening map; the class song learned by ear in unison in the head voice. Words: setting, purpose, form, verse, chorus, criteria. Showing: the chosen song sung for a partner class with the class's reasons said. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.3a, a kept card: the class's pick and three reasons (interest, knowledge, purpose or setting), with the student's own reason added; MU:Re7.1.3a, a listening-log page: how one chosen piece reflects an interest, an experience or a purpose; MU:Cn10.1.3a, a talk item: one taste, piece of knowledge or skill and the music choice it shapes (own, invented or public, marked the same); MU:Re7.2.3a, a written item on an unseen open recording: one feature of form or an element and the setting, each tied to the response; MU:Re9.1.3a, a written item on the same recording: the class checklist marked and one sentence on its fit to the setting; MU:Cn11.1.3a, a talk item: one tie between an unseen piece and another art, subject or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: note values as parts of a bar (unit 4); explaining why a setting changes a response (grade 4). Tools: the listening map, the class checklist card, the song menu cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own; a listening-map player on the teacher device or the printed map. Free path: the voice and the printed map. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First because every later unit needs the listening routine, the listening log and the class checklist (the listening-log and describe-interpret-judge models are retrieved and widened here), and because the song chosen here is the one the class reads and performs in unit 4. Listening opens the year so the class hears music in its setting before making it. Six ids, but most are heard and written, so 16 days | Candidate: a check that the three menu songs give the class a real choice of setting (a lullaby, a dance song, a game song) | Powwow songs are heard by listening only, never sung, drummed or danced by the class, and no student imitates regalia; the sensitivity agent first reads the group's and the label's pages and a Blackfeet or intertribal powwow organization's published guidance on which songs are social. The kora: the agent reads the label's page and a Mande cultural source on griot families. "Hine e Hine" is sung in te reo Maori: the agent reads a Maori source for its history and pronunciation before the unit keeps it. Own-taste tasks have an invented or public option. Hazards and controls: recordings at a set low level; voice rest and water; no student sings alone. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | 3 | Two ways to play it. *How do performers use loudness and speed to show what a piece means, and how do we make up music that fits a setting?* | 12 | mu-g3-invent; mu-g3-express | MU:Cr1.1.3a; MU:Cr1.1.3b; MU:Pr4.3.3a; MU:Re8.1.3a | Creating (Cr1); performing (Pr4); responding (Re8). Works: Claude Debussy, "Clair de lune" from Suite bergamasque (France, published 1905), a public domain score in two recordings by different pianists whose rights lines the unit plan checks; Hua Yanjun, known as Abing (China, 1893 to 1950), "Erquan Yingyue" (The Moon Reflected in the Second Spring), for erhu, recorded in 1950, described and linked to a museum's or an archive's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: improvising on purpose inside a given tonality and meter (mu-g2-invent) and two performers compared (mu-g2-express). MU:Cr1.1.3a: improvise rhythm and melody ideas on a five-note scale and describe how they fit a purpose and a setting (a game, a lullaby, a march). MU:Cr1.1.3b: invent rhythm and melody ideas inside a tonality (major or minor) or meter (twos or threes) the student chooses. MU:Pr4.3.3a: try a song with different loudness and speed and show, with a spoken reason, the version that carries its meaning. MU:Re8.1.3a: describe how two recorded performers use loudness and speed in their readings of one piece. Technique: the pentatonic call and answer, improvised on the voice or the tuned bottles; planned loudness and speed marked on the expression map. Words: improvise, tonality, meter, loudness, speed, reading. Showing: each group's two readings of one class song, played for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.3a, a performance item at the teacher's spot: a four-beat phrase improvised for a purpose card, with one sentence on how it fits; MU:Cr1.1.3b, a guide look-for on the kept idea-bank card and its sound-only recording: an idea inside the tonality or meter the student chose and named; MU:Pr4.3.3a, a guide look-for on the group's reading: the planned loudness and speed carried out, with the student's spoken reason; MU:Re8.1.3a, a written item on two unseen readings of one open-licensed tune: one loudness or speed difference and what each shows. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tone color (grade 4), harmony (grade 4). Tools: the expression map, the purpose cards and the idea-bank card, Curio Class's own; a free on-screen keyboard or the tuned bottles. Free path: the voice. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 1 because comparing performers' readings uses its listening routine and setting words; improvising for a setting needs the setting words just taught. Before unit 3 because a composed piece starts from the idea bank. One technique and four ids, so 12 days | Candidate: a third reading by a living performer, described and linked, for range | Abing was a blind street musician; his blindness is named only as his own era's published sources name it, and the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or archive's page first. Hazards and controls: tuned bottles plastic, filled and capped by an adult and taped shut; recordings at a set low level; no student improvises alone in front of the class (pairs or the teacher's spot). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | 3 | Songs with a job. *How do composers write down a piece made for a purpose, and how do we make it better?* | 16 | mu-g3-document; mu-g3-revise | MU:Cr2.1.3a; MU:Cr2.1.3b; MU:Cr3.1.3a; MU:Cr3.2.3a | Creating (Cr2, Cr3); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Elizabeth Cotten (United States, 1893 to 1987), "Freight Train", written as a girl in North Carolina and first recorded in 1958, described and linked to a folk music archive's page; Heitor Villa-Lobos, "O Trenzinho do Caipira" (The Little Train of the Caipira), from Bachianas Brasileiras No. 2 (Brazil, 1930), described and linked, its rights line checked in the unit plan: two train pieces made for two purposes | Retrieval in day 2's opener: ideas combined and put in order (mu-g2-document) and the child choosing which feedback to use (mu-g2-revise). MU:Cr2.1.3a: play chosen ideas from the idea bank as a simple short piece for a purpose (a game song, a goodnight tune, a train song) and say how they link to it. MU:Cr2.1.3b: write the piece's rhythm and melody in picture or standard notation, or record it, by beat counts and syllables (no note value as a part of a bar before unit 4). MU:Cr3.1.3a: judge and improve the piece with a checklist the teacher and class set, and mark each change on the page. MU:Cr3.2.3a: share the finished piece with an audience and describe how it shows what the student meant. Technique: writing rhythm and a three- to five-note tune on the staff, rung by rung up the notation ladder. Words: compose, purpose, notation, staff, revise, intent. Showing: the composers' share for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr2.1.3a, a performance item at the share: the piece played or sung, with its purpose said; MU:Cr2.1.3b, a guide look-for on the kept notated page (or its recording): rhythm and melody written so another student can read it; MU:Cr3.1.3a, a guide look-for on the kept change list: two changes marked against the class checklist; MU:Cr3.2.3a, a talk item after the group's turn: one way the piece shows what the student meant. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: harmony written down (grade 4). Tools: staff paper, the notation ladder card, the class checklist and change-list card, Curio Class's own; a notation tool or the printed staff. Free path: pencil and staff paper. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 2 because a piece is built from the idea bank's improvised ideas; before unit 4 because the concert asks the class to read standard notation, which writing it first prepares. The critique-protocol and notation-ladder models are retrieved here, where first needed this year. A product with a draft, a critique, a change list and a revision, so 16 days | Candidate: a piece by a living child or young composer, described and linked, beside Cotten's | Cotten wrote "Freight Train" as a child and was a domestic worker for much of her life; the unit tells her as a composer, and the sensitivity agent reads the archive's page and her own published words first. Purpose cards offer a public or invented purpose beside any family one. Hazards and controls: shared instruments wiped and hands washed; the share is in groups, never a forced solo. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | 3 | Our class concert. *How do we read, rehearse and perform a song so it fits where and why people sing it?* | 16 | mu-g3-read; mu-g3-perform | MU:Pr4.2.3a; MU:Pr4.2.3b; MU:Pr4.2.3c; MU:Pr5.1.3a; MU:Pr5.1.3b; MU:Pr6.1.3a; MU:Pr6.1.3b | Performing (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); responding practiced on the model work (review). Works: the class song chosen in unit 1, in a Curio Class arrangement with a round or a second part; Sweet Honey in the Rock (United States, an African American women's a cappella group founded in 1973; the unit plan records whether active, as of its date), "Wanting Memories" by Ysaye Maria Barnwell, described and linked to the group's page: how an ensemble blends and fits a song to its setting | Retrieval in day 2's opener: rhythm and melody patterns read with a rest (mu-g2-read) and a chosen strategy with a set checklist (mu-g2-perform). MU:Pr4.2.3a: map the form of the concert song (verse, chorus, repeat, round entries) on a form map. MU:Pr4.2.3b: read and perform its rhythms and short tune in picture and standard notation, now with note values as parts of a whole bar (after math grade 3's fractions unit). MU:Pr4.2.3c: describe how where and why people sing the song (its personal and social setting) shapes the performance. MU:Pr5.1.3a: judge a class ensemble take for accuracy with a shared checklist and feedback. MU:Pr5.1.3b: rehearse the weak spots with a named strategy to polish accuracy and expression. MU:Pr6.1.3a: perform the song expressively and accurately. MU:Pr6.1.3b: show the manners of a performer and of an audience member that suit the setting and venue. Words: form, round, note value, ensemble, rehearse, audience. Showing: the class concert for a partner class or families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: MU:Pr4.2.3a, a kept form map of the concert song, scored on the reading day; MU:Pr4.2.3b, a performance item at the teacher's spot on the reading day: an unseen four-bar rhythm and short tune read and played; MU:Pr4.2.3c, a written item on the reading day: one way the song's setting shapes how it is sung; MU:Pr5.1.3a, a kept card: a recorded class take marked on the shared checklist with one point of feedback; MU:Pr5.1.3b, a guide look-for on the kept practice card: a weak spot, its strategy and the take before and after; MU:Pr6.1.3a, a guide look-for at the concert: the part sung or played accurately and with the planned expression; MU:Pr6.1.3b, a guide look-for at the concert: performer manners in the group's turn and audience manners in the others'. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: time signatures (grade 4); solo performance (grade 4, offered here only as a choice). Tools: the form map, the practice card, the shared checklist, reading cards, Curio Class's own; a rhythm reading tool or the printed cards. Free path: the voice and printed cards. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | Last in the year because the concert ends it and because reading note values as parts of a whole bar waits for math grade 3 unit 6 (days 92 to 114, school weeks 19 to 23); this unit starts in week 25. The year-end check sits before it (week 24); its own two check days in weeks 31 and 32 are its assessment. The rhythm-syllables and practice-plan models are retrieved here | Candidate: the concert program written by the class as a kept card, read by the sensitivity agent | Family yes for any showing outside class; a student's name is printed only as the student chooses. Every part has a solo or doubled version for the home room and a group version for a student who will not sing alone. Hazards and controls: voice rest and water in each rehearsal; no loud drum near an ear; the concert space cleared and steps checked. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 4 | Music in its setting. *How does where and why music is made change how we hear it and what we choose to play?* | 16 | mu-g4-choose; mu-g4-respond | MU:Pr4.1.4a; MU:Re7.1.4a; MU:Cn10.1.4a; MU:Re7.2.4a; MU:Re9.1.4a; MU:Cn11.1.4a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: Rahim AlHaj (born 1968 in Baghdad, Iraq, lives in the United States; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a piece for oud, described and linked to the artist's page; Ang�lique Kidjo (born 1960 in Benin; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a song of her own writing, described and linked to the artist's page; Anton�n Dvor�k, Symphony No. 9, "From the New World", second movement (Largo) (written in New York by a Czech composer, 1893), a public domain score in a checked recording; the song menu: "Mo Li Hua" (Jasmine Flower, China, a folk song of the Qing dynasty), "Shenandoah" (United States, a nineteenth-century river song) and "Funicul�, Funicul�" (Italy, Luigi Denza, 1880) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: setting as a reason for a pick (mu-g3-choose) and set criteria with the setting (mu-g3-respond). MU:Pr4.1.4a: choose the class's concert piece from the menu and show how interest, knowledge, setting and the class's own technical skill (can we hold a second part?) shape the pick. MU:Re7.1.4a: show and explain on a listening-log page how a chosen piece reflects interests, experiences, purposes and contexts. MU:Cn10.1.4a: show how the student's tastes, knowledge and skills shape a choice in music, or an invented or public person's, marked the same. MU:Re7.2.4a: describe how form, elements and the social or cultural setting shape the response (Dvor�k writing in America, the oud in Iraqi music, a song sung in Fon). MU:Re9.1.4a: judge a piece by set criteria and explain whether it fits its setting. MU:Cn11.1.4a: show how music relates to another art, a school subject or daily life. Technique: listening for form and instrument families with a listening map; the concert piece learned by ear. Words: context, culture, technical skill, explain, criteria, instrument family. Showing: the chosen piece sung for a partner class with the class's argument for it. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.4a, a kept card: the pick and the reasons, one of them the class's skill; MU:Re7.1.4a, a listening-log page: how one chosen piece reflects a purpose or a context, explained; MU:Cn10.1.4a, a talk item: one taste, piece of knowledge or skill and the choice it shapes (own, invented or public, marked the same); MU:Re7.2.4a, a written item on an unseen open recording: how its form or an element and its setting shape the response, with a "because"; MU:Re9.1.4a, a written item on the same recording: set criteria marked and why it fits or does not fit its setting; MU:Cn11.1.4a, a talk item: one tie between an unseen piece and another art, subject or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: evidence cited from the music (grade 5). Tools: the listening map, the criteria card, the menu cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: the printed map. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 4 because it reopens the listening routine and the log after the summer and widens setting into culture, which units 6 and 8 use; the piece chosen here is unit 8's concert piece | Candidate: a check that each menu song's verses and history are clear before the class sings it | Dvor�k's own claims about African American spirituals and Native American music as sources are told as his claims, with Harry T. Burleigh, who sang spirituals for him, named; the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or a scholarly page first. "Shenandoah": the agent checks its verses and the class sings a checked set. AlHaj left Iraq as a refugee: told in one plain sentence from his own page, read first by the agent, and no student is asked about their own family's travels. Own-taste tasks have an invented or public option. Hazards and controls: recordings at a set low level; voice rest. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 4 | Over the drone. *How do harmony and tone color change what a song tells us?* | 12 | mu-g4-invent; mu-g4-express | MU:Cr1.1.4a; MU:Cr1.1.4b; MU:Pr4.3.4a; MU:Re8.1.4a | Creating (Cr1); performing (Pr4); responding (Re8). Works: Ulali (Native women's a cappella group; singers Pura F�, Soni Moreno and Jennifer Kreisberg, the unit plan reads each singer's nations from the group's own page and records whether active, as of its date), "Mahk Jchi" (1994), voices in close harmony over a drum heartbeat, described and linked; Anoushka Shankar (born 1981 in London to an Indian family; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a sitar performance over the tanpura drone, described and linked to the artist's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: ideas fitted to a setting in a chosen tonality (mu-g3-invent) and two readings compared (mu-g3-express). MU:Cr1.1.4a: improvise rhythm and melody over a drone, with a simple harmony part (a held note, a third above), and explain how they fit a stated purpose and setting. MU:Cr1.1.4b: invent a short accompaniment pattern (an ostinato) in a given key and meter. MU:Pr4.3.4a: plan the loudness, speed and tone color of a song on the expression map, perform it and explain the choices. MU:Re8.1.4a: explain how a recorded performer's choices of loudness, speed and tone color, and the student's own, carry the music's meaning. Technique: holding a drone or a partner part against the tune; tone color shown by voice (bright, dark, breathy) and found instruments. Words: drone, harmony, accompaniment, ostinato, tone color, interpret. Showing: each group's song over its drone and ostinato, for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.4a, a performance item at the teacher's spot: a phrase improvised over the drone, with one sentence on its fit to a purpose; MU:Cr1.1.4b, a guide look-for on the kept idea-bank card and its recording: an ostinato in the given key and meter; MU:Pr4.3.4a, a guide look-for on the group's performance and its expression map: loudness, speed and tone color as planned, with the student's spoken reason; MU:Re8.1.4a, a written item on an unseen open recording: one tone color choice and what it carries. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: chords (grade 5), articulation (grade 5). Tools: the expression map, the drone card and the idea-bank card, Curio Class's own; the free on-screen keyboard for a drone, or a sung drone. Free path: the voice and the tuned bottles. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 5 because explaining a performer's choices uses its explaining words; before unit 7 because the harmony written down there starts from the drone and ostinato ideas here. One technique and four ids, so 12 days | Candidate: a living drone tradition from Europe (a Highland bagpipe or a hurdy-gurdy player), described and linked, for range | Ulali's song: the sensitivity agent reads the group's own page and each singer's published words first, and checks that the song is the group's own, not a ceremonial song; no student imitates the drum heartbeat as a Native drum, and the class's own drone is its own. Hindustani music: a raga can carry devotional roots; the agent reads the artist's page, and the class studies it by listening only. Hazards and controls: a held drone sung softly with breaths taken in turn; headphones, if used with the keyboard tool, at a capped volume and never shared; hands washed after shared instruments. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

